g that  for what we can know about ancient  monasticism but I have a lot of  interaction with contemporary monks as  well as the Egyptian inspectors who  accompany us in our work so it's an  interesting sort of contemporary  cultural enterprise in making sure that  the relationships are sound the  communication is clear this is one of  the reasons I enjoy the work actually  how long will it take you to uncover you  know whatever it is you're uncovering I  mean I would imagine it's a large  building that's right so the residents  that we excavated starting in 2007 and  we actually just completed the  excavation this past year is about 25  meters by 25 meters it's made of mud  brick it has about 20 rooms in it  including a large courtyard in the  middle and so it took us that long six  years yeah we have a I mean our method  of doing archaeology is very painstaking  yes we're sort of taking layers and sort  of documenting not only what's in the  ground but how it came to be in the in  the form it is now so we were able to  sort of figure out how it collapsed  after it fell out of use and one of the  interesting discoveries that we've made  in this residence is one of the rooms  had extensive wall painting program as  well as a number of inscriptions a lot  of which a sort of unpainted on plaster  a lot of this plaster had fallen off the  walls prior to our coming sometime after  the building was no longer in use and so  we found these fragments of painted  plaster in the sand fill that was in the  room and our painted plaster specialist  Julian Pike piece these these fragments  together like a jigsaw puzzle and ended  up coming up with these amazing wall  paintings  of monastic saints of martyrs and so  it's we have we have an idea of when  when the monks who lived in this  residence and we know the dating of this  residence from the inscriptions way too  dated and inscriptions from the 10th  century so we know a little bit about  when they walked into this room what  they would have seen the sort of  interaction between monks coming into  the room to pray for example and what  they would have seen on the walls where  different figures sort of models for  their own piety would have been located  what they would been able to read on the  walls dedicatory inscription prayers etc  so you must have to have to have a lot  of patience and then how exciting to  have something to be completely covered  and then you uncover it and then you  know six years later you're able to see  you know what this structure was all  about I mean it must be fascinating it's  a fascinating discovery but there's also  it's interesting it's also frustration  so you just narrated this process of  sort of uncovering actually after every  season we cover it up again we backfill  this is a building that is made of mud  brick and basically the best way that we  can preserve it is to take the sand that  we have excavated sifted as now clean  sand and backfill it into the space if  we would leave this building exposed  because of the the elements the Sun the  wind Kate very occasionally the rain it  would suit you would soon see the  results of deterioration and there are  examples in the area of structures that  have been excavated previously by other  teams where they have left them open and  you know and there's also sometimes  human interventions vandalism and things  like that so in that sense we don't  always get the gratification of at that  particular site at least of seeing it  sort of displayed and this is one of the  challenges in archaeology because our  responsibility is not only the  investigation the excavation itself but  it's also figuring out how to be  responsible with this cultural heritage  and sometimes that means very specific  conservation methods so that we can  display it and other times where that's  beyond our ability at this point or  beyond the level of expense that we can  can do and so we take the best approach  that we can to make sure that for future  investigators they want to come back in  they can find this building in good  shape and do their own studies  interesting so let's talk a bit about  the findings and the implications of  those findings at both of the sites sure  so I've said a little bit about this  residence I've talked about the wall  paintings but there's other things that  are interesting about this residence  there's a number of kitchen  installations in this residence and from  those kitchen installations we have  gotten basically leavings from the ovens  that we have done archeo botanical  analysis on and we can get an idea of  the kind of diet just amongst have  actual pieces of food yeah sort of like  remains of grapes green Wow beans I mean  we have an idea of some of some of what  they ingested I'm just thinking it's all  mixed in with sand and you have to like  extract the beans and the grains from  the sand that must be yeah there's  particular method methods of location  and to in order to separate the sand  from these other elements so that's one  example on another example from that  particular building is we see the ways  that the the monks renovated their their  spaces so you know we just did yeah  we've gone through this whole process of  renovating buildings at Yale the  colleges have gone through yes in  renovations well the monks were doing  this with their own buildings they were  sometimes walls would become would sort  of semi collapse or become weak and they  would reinforce it so we have really  thick walls they would close up windows  they would open up rooms so it's a you  see the building as a kind of dynamic  living object and the way that the monks  sort of interacted with their space so  that's that's the site in the north in  the South we've been doing a variety of  things and I mentioned a small church  with a tomb under it and probably our  most surprising discovery came out of  that this was a church that had been  excavated by  the Egyptian inspectors starting in 2002  when we came in we cooperated with them  and then did some further excavations as  well as conservation of what remained  there's some wall paintings but within  the tomb it's a very small space and one  of the things that we did was conserve  the clean and conserve the the paintings  that we found in there and if a couple  of years ago after a stage of that  cleaning we we kind of came to a  discovery on one wall of this spate this  tomb space is an image of a human human  image and it's an image of shenouda who  was the famous leader of this monastery  back in the 5th century and as an  inscription and accompanying actually in  greek accompanying this this figure and  he's he's standing and he's holding a  crown in one of his hands and his other  hand arm is partially preserved he would  have been holding a crown there and  after this cleaning stage we realized  that he was flanked by two figures  originally we thought they might have  been saints but you could see edges of  wings so these figures were angels and  this the placement of a human figure  between angels in this particular  posture is very much related to funerary  representation and so that combined with  the content of the inscription led us to  conclude that it's very likely that this  particular tomb was prepared for this  famous figure and we sort of came up out  of the tomb and looked at each other and  realize way wait a minute we couldn't  believe that this that that's what the  conclusion we were drawing so that was a  very surprising but wonderful discovery  what there's also a church there my  colleague Bentley Layton in the  Religious Studies department has been  doing architectural documentation on  that church and as part of that work we  were trying to clarify some  architectural elements and we wanted to  excavate a niche in one of the rooms a  sort of upstairs in this church and in  doing that we need  to clean and excavate the floor and in  doing that we found we ran across  manuscript fragments one of the things  that this monk shenouda was known for  was producing the largest corpus of  literature and Coptic that we have and  there's an international project right  now editing his works and at we knew  that a lot of these works were  originally kept somewhere in this church  and had been disseminated like in the  eighteenth nineteenth centuries all over  the world so we in order to study him  you have to go to like Moscow in Paris  Cairo and all these different places but  we had never been absolutely sure where  these texts have been kept where the  sort of archived webinars to confirm  that at least for a particular period of  time this room was used to store those  manuscripts some of the matchup and so  they essentially when when when people  in the monks had had taken the  manuscripts out of that room some of the  brittle edges had broken off and so that  was that was another really neat  discovery right right so I understand  that you are not done there that you  will continue work in both places yes do  you have any thoughts as to what you may  find or perhaps what you would hope to  find sure in the South the white  monastery one of our one of the things  it is not so much a finding but one of  our responsibilities is to make sure  that the the church remains in good  shape their parts of the church that are  hazardous so this this citrus is still  in use by the Coptic community and yet  their spaces where walls are kind of  threatening to fall down and so one of  the things that we have going on at  least next is is some consolidation work  on that at our other site we're in the  process of preparing a book documenting  the excavation of this this resonance  but we have about 70  buildings did you choose from and so  what I would like to find I would like  to work on is getting some comparison  looking at some other resonances seeing  what other art historical evidence is  yielded from that one of the things  about doing monastic archaeology is we  simply there simply has not been a lot  of it done you know handful of projects  over the years and why do you think that  is well there's a there's an interesting  history in archaeology especially in  Egypt so traditional sort of classical  archaeology in Egypt was always looking  for the sort of Veronica mains let's go  to the front of temples and a lot of the  later historical layers Coptic and  Islamic layers were sort of layered over  those remains and so the archaeologists  would simply sort of dig through an  early discard a lot of this valuable  historical evidence to get to what they  wanted to get to right and and so and so  I think it's important to pause and look  at these later periods to see what sorts  of developments were taking place in the  way that people were living and the way  that people were were dying the way that  people were burying loved ones right  within monasteries so that's one of the  one of the things I think is it was  really important about this work and it  gives us a real kind of textured sense  of everyday life from you know the the  lading ancient period 4th 5th 6th  centuries up to the medieval period ok  well thank you so much for being here  with us today it's been fascinating and  we'll look for more work to come yeah  for sure ok for more information about  professor Davis and his research please  visit our website at yale edu slash  mcmillan report be sure to join us again  for another episode of the macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at Yale  you

